=5"}], "model": {"prior_margin": 8.61, "prior_sd": 5.0, "poll_margin": 10.44, "poll_sd": 4.54, "poll_weight": 0.55, "fundamentals": {"margin": 7.12, "lean": -1.0, "environment": 7.56, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": 0.56}, "rating_margin": 9.6, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.7, "baseline_margin": 9.62, "analyst_adjustment": -0.12, "key_factors": ["Cooper's large polling lead (~9-10 points) across many pollsters", "GOP outside groups pulling spending and Whatley pausing ads", "Cooper's dominant fundraising advantage ($35M raised, $20.7M on hand)", "Open seat in a slightly Republican-leaning state"], "rationale": "Polls consistently show Cooper ahead by roughly nine to ten points, raters unanimously call the race a Democratic flip, and markets price Cooper near 96-97%. Republican super PACs have pulled money out and Whatley has paused advertising, signaling a collapsing GOP effort.", "watch": "A late Republican spending re-entry or polling error toward Whatley could narrow the margin, though the current evidence makes that unlikely.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Thom Tillis (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's open Senate seat has moved decisively toward Democrat Roy Cooper, a two-term governor running against Republican Michael Whatley in a state that leans slightly Republican at the baseline. Polls from a wide range of pollsters, including Republican-affiliated firms, show Cooper leading by margins from five to nineteen points, with averages clustering near nine to ten.\n\nThe race's trajectory is reflected in Republican behavior: major outside groups have paused or withdrawn spending, Senate Republicans have shifted money to other states, and Whatley's campaign has pulled its television ads. Cooper has also vastly out-raised Whatley. Markets and nearly all expert raters treat Cooper as the strong favorite to flip the seat.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9425, "margin": 7.94}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0